#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054411 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c05c87-f04c-5f15-96f6-45287dd45876" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations that Require Subtraction"}] :question/id #uuid "ca811d3a-a7be-536b-b997-dd2355cb00d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12999" :question/problem "Find the solution to the equation $16 + x =10$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the left-hand-side so that it's similar to what we've seen before.\n\n$$\nx + 16 = 10\n$$\n\nTo isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite* of adding $16$ to $x$, which is *subtracting* $16$ from $x$. Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x + 16 &= 10 \\\\ x + 16 - 16 &= 10 - 16 \\\\ x + 0 &= -6 \\\\ x &= -6 \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054412 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3163647c-e7ba-545e-b4a7-a8a3fff2125f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations that Require Addition"}] :question/id #uuid "094ae644-0ce0-51e7-849f-38320770df93" :question/math-academy-id "e-13000" :question/problem "Solve the equation $x-8 =6$." :question/worked-solution "To isolate $x$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $8$ from $x$, which is *adding* $8$ to $x$. Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x-8 &= 6 \\\\ x-8+8 &= 6+8 \\\\ x+0 &= 14 \\\\ x &= 14 \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054413 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054783 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51a61370-93c1-503c-bb53-af6442794f20" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations Containing Negative Constants"}] :question/id #uuid "2d414c47-6dff-54f2-a23a-ee071f8f43fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-13001" :question/problem "What is the solution to the equation $-15 = p - 12$?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\np-12 = -15\n$$\n\nTo isolate $p$, we need to perform the *opposite* of subtracting $12$ from $p$, which is *adding* $12$ to $p$. Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} p-12 &= -15 \\\\ p-12 + 12 &= -15 + 12 \\\\ p+0 &=-3 \\\\ p &=-3 \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054414 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82b22101-e915-51b6-a5f3-78a1f286c2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations Containing Non-Integer Constants"}] :question/id #uuid "20d2de55-4713-570b-a5df-dc1a52b6fbc3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13002" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{3}{4} + z$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2} &= \\frac{3}{4} + z \\\\\n\\frac{3}{4} + z &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nz + \\frac{3}{4} &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo isolate $z$, we need to perform the *opposite* of adding\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nto $z$, which is *subtracting*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nfrom $z$. Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz + \\frac{3}{4} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nz + \\frac{3}{4} - \\frac{3}{4} &= \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\nz + 0 &= \\frac{2}{4} - \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\nz &= \\frac{2 - 3}{4} \\\\\nz &= -\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
